> [@seanmonstar](https://github.com/seanmonstar) I'm so sorry about this! I'm going to look into it.
> 
> My best guess is that I botched some coordination between our readiness to write a body and the Writers ability to be written to and it's somehow hot polling. What I don't understand though is why the backing out after 16 loops isn't protecting something this severe from happening...
> 
> If [@mattklein123](https://github.com/mattklein123) [@18o](https://github.com/18o) [@mattklein123](https://github.com/mattklein123) or [@shikhar](https://github.com/shikhar) can provide a minimum repro based off of 1.8.0 that would be a huge help.
> 
> And just to clarify, your server is sending down a large stream, correct?
> 
> Is everyone using Tokio? Any other details would helpful even if you can't provide a repro.
> 
> Thanks!

Yes, I'm using Tokio as the runtime. My scenario doesn't involve handling a single large stream; instead, I'm processing 300 streams simultaneously, each around 100MB in size.  The issue was resolved once hyper was updated to version 1.8.1.  

For example, a minimal example like this:

```
use reqwest::Client;
use salvo::prelude::Stream;
use reqwest::header::{CONTENT_DISPOSITION, CONTENT_TYPE};

pub async fn download_and_stream(url: &str, res: &mut salvo::Response) -> Result<(), String> {
    let client = Client::new();
    let response = client.get(url).send().await.map_err(|e| format!("Request failed: {}", e))?;

    if response.status().as_u16() != 200 {
        return Err(format!("HTTP error: {}", response.status()));
    }

    let content_type = response
        .headers()
        .get(CONTENT_TYPE)
        .and_then(|v| v.to_str().ok())
        .unwrap_or("application/octet-stream");

    let content_length = response.content_length().unwrap_or(0);

    res.headers_mut().insert(CONTENT_TYPE, content_type.parse().unwrap());
    if content_length > 0 {
        res.headers_mut().insert("Content-Length", content_length.into());
    }
    res.headers_mut().insert(
        CONTENT_DISPOSITION,
        "attachment; filename=download".parse().unwrap()
    );

    res.stream(response.bytes_stream());

    Ok(())
}
```
